Meteoroclast Gidra
Ingredients: SURF ROCK + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Synth Strings
Creative Catalyst: its instruments are built from meteorite fragments and antique clockwork.

It sounds like a rusted iron tidal wave crashing against a sheer concrete monolith, driven by spring-reverb tremolo pickings made of space-rock slag and the heavy, grinding gears of century-old escapement wheels. Underneath the furious twang, weeping, uncalibrated synth strings groan with thermal expansion, creating a sound that is simultaneously oceanic, architectural, and violently metallic. 

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 155–170 BPM, relentless jackhammer surf-beats punctuated by syncopated clockwork hiccups and raw concrete stomps.
- Harmonic Palette: Diminished microtonal scales derived from the crystalline structure of fallen iron-nickel meteorites, clashing with traditional surf major keys.
- Production Signatures: 1) Direct-in-board overloading of tube preamps using current generated by hand-wound copper clocksprings. 2) Natural acoustic reverb captured inside hollowed-out brutalist elevator shafts. 3) Tape-saturation warp caused by thermal heat leaking off friction-heavy escapement mechanisms.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions from desolate, ticking lulls to blistering, walls-of-sound tidal climaxes that threaten to shatter the speakers.

Scene & Origin Lore
Forged in the subterranean concrete fallout shelters beneath coastal seismic observatories, this genre was birthed by rogue horologists and beach-shack castaways who scavenged space debris from the 1974 coastal meteor shower. Wearing lead-lined wetsuits bolted together with brass gears and pocket-watch casings, the subculture gathers at midnight on eroded cliffs to worship the "Great Mainspring." Their rituals involve synchronizing metronomes to the rhythmic pounding of rogue waves before unleashing deafening, gear-grinding cascades of sound that mock the passage of time.

Typical Instruments
- The Astro-Trem Guitars (custom offset guitars with bodies carved from solid chondrite meteorite and pickups wrapped in antique pendulum wire)
- The Tick-Harmonium (a brutally heavy, hand-cranked synth-string apparatus driven by grandfather clock weights)
- Concrete Bass (a massive tension-cable stretched across a raw brutalist pillar and amplified through copper stovepipes)
- Escapement Snare (a snare drum lined with spinning gears that physically shred the drumsticks as they strike)

